"""Make the signed bpm index, upatch tree and shield catalog of a registry namespace hold what main publishes.

    tools/publish --registry HOST --namespace NS --dry-run
    tools/publish --registry HOST --namespace NS --cert FILE --signer CMD --shirudo-src DIR

Published: each shield's newest version, unless it or the shield is
withdrawn. <NS>/bpm-index:stable then lists exactly those packages at
<NS>/<package>; every other entry is removed.

The signed index names each package by the repository hosts pull it from,
<PUB>/<package>: --public-namespace, by default the namespace of
--tree-endpoint (oci://<host>/<PUB>/upatch-tree), else <NS>. Pushes go to
<NS>; the host serving <PUB> maps it there.

  1. tools/validate must pass.
  2. Diff the live index (+ add, - remove, = keep). A kept version is
     immutable: its Beefile, README.md and files must equal this tree's.
     --dry-run stops here. Nothing to change: stop.
  3. tools/build, then `shirudo-build bpm build` per package (--network
     none), signed through --signer. Kept versions must rebuild to the
     same upatches, files and signer; their live .bee is pushed again.
  4. Push to a loopback staging registry (the first push --new-index, so
     shirudo-build writes and signs the index), check it, check the live
     index has not moved, copy everything over, the index tag last.
  5. Update the upatch tree hosts follow with `bpm tree sync`
     (tools/treepub.py), in one atomic move of <NS>/upatch-tree:stable.
  6. Push catalog/shield-registry.json as <NS>/shield-catalog:stable when
     it differs from the live one. The catalog is not signed: installers
     read it for names, distro fixes and relevance, never for which build
     loads (that is the signed index's).

Registry credentials: PUBLISH_REGISTRY_USERNAME, PUBLISH_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
(used here only, never passed to a container). There is no key file:
--signer is a SHIRUDO_SIGNER command (e.g. a KMS signer) that reads the
bytes to sign on stdin and writes the raw ML-DSA-87 signature. It runs on
the host; the containers reach it through a Unix socket relay.
"""

